## 6. Sampling Design and Measurements

Nine composite bottom-sediment samples were collected from the Escravos River bed using a grab sampler. Sampling was carried out along the approximately 28.5 km study stretch.

Each of the nine sampling locations has a geographic coordinate and recorded sampling depth.

| Station | Sampling Location                        | Depth (m) | Latitude (DMS) | Longitude (DMS) |
| ------- | ---------------------------------------- | --------: | -------------- | --------------- |
| 1       | Jones' Creek Tributary                   |      3.58 | 05°38'20.7"N   | 005°24'22.3"E   |
| 2       | Okerenkoko (Debama Quarters) Water Front |      2.55 | 05°37'59.8"N   | 005°23'44.3"E   |
| 3       | Okerenkoko University Jetty Water Front  |      3.22 | 05°37'08.1"N   | 005°23'24.8"E   |
| 4       | Seitobobor Polo                          |      5.83 | 05°35'38.0"N   | 005°23'43.1"E   |
| 5       | Kurutie                                  |      2.82 | 05°34'58.2"N   | 005°20'56.2"E   |
| 6       | Areton                                   |      8.21 | 05°36'16.2"N   | 005°11'35.7"E   |
| 7       | Escravos Water Front                     |      6.25 | 05°36'22.4"N   | 005°11'46.7"E   |
| 8       | Benin River Junction                     |      9.77 | 05°37'39.9"N   | 005°17'34.9"E   |
| 9       | Benikrukru Entrance                      |      5.50 | 05°38'01.1"N   | 005°17'35.8"E   |

The sediment samples were prepared and sent to the **University of the Witwatersrand, Johannesburg, South Africa**, for ICP-MS analysis.

Each of the nine sediment samples was analysed for the same six elements. Therefore:

**9 sampling locations × 6 measured elements = 54 elemental concentration measurements.**

The six measured elements are:

| Element   | Symbol | Unit  |
| --------- | ------ | ----- |
| Thorium   | Th     | mg/kg |
| Uranium   | U      | mg/kg |
| Niobium   | Nb     | mg/kg |
| Rubidium  | Rb     | mg/kg |
| Barium    | Ba     | mg/kg |
| Zirconium | Zr     | mg/kg |

Thorium and uranium are naturally radioactive elements. Nb, Rb, Ba and Zr are treated as associated geochemical elements for the purposes of this GIS assessment.

